WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.720
A typical human cell with a nucleus holds nearly 2 metres of DNA.

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:15.760
It is packed into a nucleus only about 0.005 to 0.010 millimetres across.

3
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.800
Before a typical cell divides, it copies roughly 6 billion base pairs.

4
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.320
It gets almost every one right.

5
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.720
How can the structure of DNA store information and copy itself almost perfectly?

6
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:33.483
DNA is a long chain built from small repeating units.

7
00:00:34.303 --> 00:00:35.583
Each unit is a nucleotide.

8
00:00:36.423 --> 00:00:41.563
A nucleotide has three parts: a sugar called deoxyribose, a phosphate group, and a

9
00:00:41.563 --> 00:00:43.843
base. The base carries information.

10
00:00:44.963 --> 00:00:49.323
DNA uses four bases: adenine, thymine, cytosine and guanine.

11
00:00:50.163 --> 00:00:51.863
We write them A, T, C and G.

12
00:00:52.817 --> 00:00:58.417
A covalent bond, a strong bond in which atoms share electrons, joins each sugar to

13
00:00:58.417 --> 00:00:59.417
the next phosphate.

14
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:02.817
Two strands face each other, with their bases inside.

15
00:01:03.717 --> 00:01:08.657
A hydrogen bond is a weak attraction, and hydrogen bonds hold each pair of bases

16
00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:14.337
together. Base pairing is the rule that always pairs with T, and C always pairs with

17
00:01:14.337 --> 00:01:18.097
G. Together the two strands twist into a double helix.

18
00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.650
Because of base pairing, each strand holds everything needed to rebuild its partner.

19
00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.690
An enzyme is a molecule, usually a protein, that speeds up a reaction without being

20
00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.710
used up. One enzyme unwinds the helix and unzips it.

21
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.710
The hydrogen bonds between the base pairs break.

22
00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:38.750
The backbones stay whole.

23
00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:44.503
Each old strand now works as a template, a pattern that sets the order of a new

24
00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:47.243
strand. Free nucleotides float nearby.

25
00:01:48.143 --> 00:01:53.863
Each one pairs with its matching base: A with T, C with G. Another enzyme, called

26
00:01:53.863 --> 00:01:57.883
DNA polymerase, joins the new nucleotides with covalent bonds.

27
00:01:58.843 --> 00:02:00.603
The result is two DNA molecules.

28
00:02:01.643 --> 00:02:03.963
Each has one old strand and one new strand.

29
00:02:04.967 --> 00:02:08.447
Both new molecules have the same base order as the original.

30
00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:13.027
So the order of the bases is the information, and copying keeps it.

31
00:02:13.887 --> 00:02:16.867
Base pairing allows only one correct partner at each spot.

32
00:02:17.787 --> 00:02:19.507
Proofreading takes out most wrong bases.

33
00:02:20.407 --> 00:02:25.627
A textbook estimate is about one uncorrected mistake for every billion bases copied.
